{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfZNarA5d5KNKbiXt8Y5a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The candidate `personal_ideal_alternation` passed the self-check (`OK`). On the smoke fit its ELPD-LOO was −1009.8; the current best model, `local_representativeness`, has −1398.4.\n\nThat comparison is only a rough signal. The smoke fit was short (100 draws, 1 chain), and it flagged two issues:\n- **PSIS-LOO looks unreliable:** 1.4% of trials have high Pareto-k, above the 1.0% tolerance, so that ELPD may be inaccurate.\n- **Convergence is untested:** R-hat can't be computed from one chain. Admission's full fit still has to meet the convergence gate and the 30-minute limit, and novelty wasn't checked either.\n\n**Hypothesis:** each person has their own ideal rate at which consecutive flips should differ. A sequence looks more random the closer its share of switches is to that person's ideal, and the ideals vary between people. This is aimed at the one critique finding that survived the FDR correction: people differ in how much they prefer alternating sequences far more than the best model produces (observed spread 0.217 vs 0.053).\n\n**Where it should disagree most with `local_representativeness`:**\n- **Highly alternating sequences** (e.g. HTHTHTHT versus a streaky one): it predicts some people strongly prefer them and others strongly reject them.\n- **Pairs that differ in balance but not in switch rate:** it predicts people are indifferent.\n\n**Model:**\n- Each participant's ideal is drawn from a population distribution on the logit scale, set up in the non-centred form that usually samples more reliably.\n- A single sensitivity parameter scales the squared distance from the ideal.\n- `p_left` is a sigmoid of the difference in those distances, used directly as the Bernoulli probability.\n\n**Risk for later experiments:** there are 400 participant slots, so participant ids must stay below 400; a higher i